1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:05,000
Take some milk, add a little bacteria,

2
00:00:05,001 --> 00:00:08,000
some mold, maybe a few mites.

3
00:00:08,001 --> 00:00:11,000
Cheese is just a means of a controlled spoilage.

4
00:00:11,001 --> 00:00:14,000
And you get a delicacy that people can't get enough of.

5
00:00:14,001 --> 00:00:19,000
From pasture to pizza, from Roquefort to Velveeta.

6
00:00:19,001 --> 00:00:22,000
No matter how you slice it or spray it,

7
00:00:22,001 --> 00:00:26,000
it's one of humankind's most complex and cherished foods.

8
00:00:26,001 --> 00:00:27,660
and cherished foods.

9
00:00:27,661 --> 00:00:30,000
Now, cheese on Modern Marvels.

10
00:00:43,180 --> 00:00:46,800
The trucks begin arriving before dawn.

11
00:00:46,801 --> 00:00:51,240
Gleaming steel tankers, each one hauling 6,000 gallons,

12
00:00:51,241 --> 00:00:53,600
roll into the offloading bays.

13
00:00:53,601 --> 00:00:56,060
It is a refinery of sorts.

14
00:00:56,061 --> 00:00:59,100
But the commodity here isn't oil or natural gas.

15
00:00:59,101 --> 00:01:01,980
It's milk.

16
00:01:01,981 --> 00:01:05,220
This is the Alto Dairy in Waupon, Wisconsin.

17
00:01:05,221 --> 00:01:07,440
A typical day, there's about 3 and 1

18
00:01:07,441 --> 00:01:11,420
half million pounds of milk that come into the plant.

19
00:01:11,421 --> 00:01:14,020
So it's about 70 truckloads.

20
00:01:14,021 --> 00:01:17,520
The Alto Dairy takes those 3.5 million pounds of milk

21
00:01:17,521 --> 00:01:21,900
and begins turning them into 400,000 pounds of cheese

22
00:01:21,901 --> 00:01:23,400
every day.

23
00:01:23,401 --> 00:01:27,340
90% of the milk produced in Wisconsin goes into cheese.

24
00:01:27,341 --> 00:01:29,800
And fully 10% of Wisconsin's cheese

25
00:01:29,801 --> 00:01:32,500
gets made here at Alto, the largest cheese plant

26
00:01:32,501 --> 00:01:35,580
east of the Mississippi.

27
00:01:35,581 --> 00:01:38,440
From giant factories to small farms,

28
00:01:38,441 --> 00:01:44,020
making cheese is an art, a science, and a craft.

29
00:01:44,021 --> 00:01:50,820
It's also a relationship between land, animal, and technique.

30
00:01:50,821 --> 00:01:52,560
A human being is just another one

31
00:01:52,561 --> 00:01:56,400
or six factors that are integral to the science

32
00:01:56,500 --> 00:02:00,000
and art of cheese making.

33
00:02:00,100 --> 00:02:03,600
At the Alto Dairy, as with any cheese factory,

34
00:02:03,700 --> 00:02:07,800
good cheese begins with a pure, consistent milk supply.

35
00:02:07,900 --> 00:02:10,620
First thing he does when the truck pulls in

36
00:02:10,720 --> 00:02:13,620
is the intake guy opens up the top cover

37
00:02:13,720 --> 00:02:15,920
and gets a sample of the milk out.

38
00:02:16,020 --> 00:02:17,820
It's checked for antibiotics.

39
00:02:17,920 --> 00:02:21,560
Once the lab clears it, they hook the hose on,

40
00:02:21,561 --> 00:02:25,800
on and they start to unload the truck.

41
00:02:25,801 --> 00:02:29,360
The dairy pumps the milk into giant holding silos.

42
00:02:29,361 --> 00:02:31,640
When a batch of cheese begins, the milk

43
00:02:31,641 --> 00:02:34,120
heads to the pasteurizer, where it's quickly heated

44
00:02:34,121 --> 00:02:40,320
to about 162 degrees Fahrenheit, killing any bacteria.

45
00:02:40,321 --> 00:02:43,320
Then it's on to the cheese making back.

46
00:02:43,321 --> 00:02:45,400
Ironically, the next and most crucial step

47
00:02:45,401 --> 00:02:49,520
in making any cheese, from a creamy fresh cheese

48
00:02:49,521 --> 00:02:54,220
to an aged parmesan is adding bacteria,

49
00:02:54,221 --> 00:02:57,560
the so-called good bacteria known as the starter culture.

50
00:02:57,561 --> 00:02:59,320
We have to put good bacteria back in,

51
00:02:59,321 --> 00:03:02,460
and that's the starter culture we just dumped in.

52
00:03:02,461 --> 00:03:05,060
And what that starter does is the bacteria

53
00:03:05,061 --> 00:03:09,980
start to eat the lactose or the milk sugars.

54
00:03:09,981 --> 00:03:13,320
As the bacteria, aided by moderate heat and agitation,

55
00:03:13,321 --> 00:03:17,020
consume the sugars and ferment them into lactic acid,

56
00:03:17,021 --> 00:03:18,720
they lower the pH balance of the milk.

57
00:03:19,860 --> 00:03:22,980
This will eventually give the cheese its acidic, tangy flavor.

58
00:03:24,380 --> 00:03:28,100
The pH drop also prepares the milk proteins to coagulate.

59
00:03:29,540 --> 00:03:32,380
This particular batch of milk is destined to become cheddar.

60
00:03:33,240 --> 00:03:34,900
So before the milk is coagulated,

61
00:03:35,440 --> 00:03:38,040
the cheesemakers add a vegetable dye called annatto

62
00:03:38,041 --> 00:03:40,660
to give the cheddar its trademark orange color.

63
00:03:41,780 --> 00:03:45,440
The actual coagulation is made possible by a miraculous enzyme

64
00:03:45,441 --> 00:03:46,642
known as rennet.

65
00:03:46,643 --> 00:03:52,200
Although today's cheese makers often use rennet synthesized from vegetable

66
00:03:52,201 --> 00:03:58,200
sources, it was originally taken from the stomach lining of ruminant animals, such as cows and sheep.

67
00:04:00,220 --> 00:04:04,480
In just 30 minutes, a mere drizzle of rennet turns this giant vat of milk

68
00:04:04,481 --> 00:04:09,920
into a yogurt-like mass. In 55,000 pounds of milk,

69
00:04:09,921 --> 00:04:13,600
we're using about 70 ounces of rennet. It doesn't take much.

70
00:04:13,601 --> 00:04:20,600
Automatic knives then carefully cut through the solid portion, known as curd, and expel

71
00:04:20,601 --> 00:04:24,560
the liquid, known as whey.

72
00:04:24,561 --> 00:04:30,720
This is another universal aspect of cheese making at dairies large and small.

73
00:04:30,721 --> 00:04:34,980
The size of the curds will determine the texture and moisture content of the cheese.

74
00:04:34,981 --> 00:04:40,280
The finer the curd size, the more whey that's expelled.

75
00:04:40,281 --> 00:04:43,960
is cut very fine, which will lead to a dry, firm cheese.

76
00:04:44,240 --> 00:04:48,260
We'll then wash the curd, which lowers the temperature

77
00:04:48,261 --> 00:04:50,260
so the bacteria will slow down,

78
00:04:50,700 --> 00:04:52,420
plus remove some of their food source

79
00:04:52,421 --> 00:04:54,360
by washing out some lactose.

80
00:04:55,100 --> 00:04:56,720
And then we will salt the cheese,

81
00:04:56,780 --> 00:04:59,560
and the salt, again, is a way to not only flavor it,

82
00:04:59,561 --> 00:05:00,500
to help give it the flavor,

83
00:05:00,600 --> 00:05:03,240
but it helps to control the bacteria.

84
00:05:05,020 --> 00:05:07,400
The curd is piped to the cheese press area,

85
00:05:07,720 --> 00:05:09,780
where it will fill one of these forms,

86
00:05:09,781 --> 00:05:16,000
known in the trade as a 640, since it will form a 640-pound block of cheese.

87
00:05:16,540 --> 00:05:22,420
The box is then pushed into the drainer presses, and it's allowed to stand for five minutes.

88
00:05:25,120 --> 00:05:29,320
After the five minutes of stand time, now the drainer press is lowered.

89
00:05:30,180 --> 00:05:32,880
The entire press will then tip down for weight drainage.

90
00:05:33,840 --> 00:05:39,360
Each individual box is pressed for about eight minutes with 55 pounds of PSI.

91
00:05:39,780 --> 00:05:44,280
This hydraulically powered machine is an industrial version of the cheese press,

92
00:05:44,281 --> 00:05:50,280
a simple tool used by cheesemakers for thousands of years to shape the curd and press out way.

93
00:05:50,281 --> 00:05:57,780
The forms are further pressurized in a vacuum chamber.

94
00:05:57,781 --> 00:06:06,780
Over time, the curd pieces fuse into what we'll recognize as cheddar cheese.

95
00:06:06,781 --> 00:06:09,780
Meanwhile, deep inside the cheese,

96
00:06:09,781 --> 00:06:12,780
the starter bacteria continue to break the milk sugars

97
00:06:12,781 --> 00:06:16,780
into tangy lactic acid and proteins

98
00:06:16,781 --> 00:06:19,780
into sharply flavored volatile compounds,

99
00:06:19,781 --> 00:06:22,780
gradually imparting flavor to the cheese.

100
00:06:22,781 --> 00:06:25,780
And that's what the process of this controlled spoilage

101
00:06:25,781 --> 00:06:27,780
of making cheese is all about,

102
00:06:27,781 --> 00:06:30,780
having those molecules break down into simpler and simpler

103
00:06:30,781 --> 00:06:33,780
molecules so that the cheese can take life.

104
00:06:33,781 --> 00:06:37,780
Most of the aging will have to take place away from Alto

105
00:06:37,781 --> 00:06:41,780
Dairy, which ripens the cheddars in cold storage for only 10

106
00:06:41,781 --> 00:06:45,780
days before moving the giant blocks out the door to its

107
00:06:45,781 --> 00:06:49,780
wholesale customers. And so goes this high-volume,

108
00:06:49,781 --> 00:06:53,780
high-tech version of a classic English farmhouse cheese.

109
00:06:53,781 --> 00:06:57,780
This is an English farmhouse cheddar.

110
00:06:57,781 --> 00:07:01,780
This is a New York cheddar. All cheddar really is white.

111
00:07:01,781 --> 00:07:08,780
really is white. All cheese really starts out white. And keep in mind, cheddar was a

112
00:07:08,781 --> 00:07:10,313
town in England, right?

113
00:07:10,314 --> 00:07:14,780
Our tradition of making cheese a great deal comes from the English,

114
00:07:14,781 --> 00:07:18,387
and that's why we make a lot of cheddar here in the United States.

115
00:07:18,388 --> 00:07:19,480
As a matter of fact,

116
00:07:19,481 --> 00:07:25,780
we make more cheese here in the United States than any other country.

117
00:07:25,781 --> 00:07:30,038
Which is saying something because the world makes a lot of cheese.

118
00:07:30,039 --> 00:07:31,780
From Munster to mozzarella,

119
00:07:31,781 --> 00:07:33,780
Ricotta to Roquefort.

120
00:07:33,781 --> 00:07:38,780
The world's cheesemakers produce 20 million tons of this stuff in a year.

121
00:07:38,781 --> 00:07:44,780
More than all the world's tobacco, coffee beans, tea leaves, and cocoa beans combined.

122
00:07:44,781 --> 00:07:48,780
But cheese cannot be measured in volume alone.

123
00:07:48,781 --> 00:07:52,780
It's an enduring obsession, a seductive pleasure.

124
00:07:52,781 --> 00:07:57,780
Whether it's a pungent slice of Brindamur or a bite of a cheeseburger.

125
00:07:57,781 --> 00:08:00,680
of a cheeseburger.

126
00:08:00,681 --> 00:08:02,480
As a portable protein-filled food,

127
00:08:02,481 --> 00:08:05,020
it's also a key element in the story of human survival.

128
00:08:08,120 --> 00:08:12,860
We can absolutely authenticate that there was cheese making

129
00:08:12,861 --> 00:08:17,000
going on in the Basque Country, 3000 BC,

130
00:08:17,001 --> 00:08:18,760
and certainly in the area that we know now

131
00:08:18,761 --> 00:08:23,040
as Turkey and Iran, Syria, probably thousands of years

132
00:08:23,041 --> 00:08:25,140
before the Basque Country.

133
00:08:25,141 --> 00:08:27,100
But the most concrete example that we have

134
00:08:27,101 --> 00:08:31,020
is that residue that archaeologists found in an earthenware pot

135
00:08:31,021 --> 00:08:34,820
in what we know now as Egypt in 2300 B.C.

136
00:08:34,900 --> 00:08:37,860
Somewhere between 2300 B.C. and 3000 B.C. was carbon dated.

137
00:08:39,320 --> 00:08:42,920
Somewhere between scholarly historical conjecture and legend

138
00:08:42,921 --> 00:08:45,940
is the theory that cheesemaking began by accident,

139
00:08:46,640 --> 00:08:48,220
thanks to nomadic shepherds,

140
00:08:48,500 --> 00:08:50,220
who happened to carry their milk in a bag

141
00:08:50,221 --> 00:08:52,060
made from the stomach of a goat or sheep.

142
00:08:53,080 --> 00:08:56,740
As they carried it with them, the milk would react, for example,

143
00:08:56,741 --> 00:08:59,080
to the stomach lining, which has the rennet in it,

144
00:08:59,280 --> 00:09:01,420
and cause the curd to separate from the whey.

145
00:09:01,760 --> 00:09:03,700
And what they're left with are those little curds.

146
00:09:04,240 --> 00:09:07,040
And if you pack that together, that's cheese.

147
00:09:07,720 --> 00:09:12,120
And very little has changed in that 5,000, 6,000, 7,000, 8,000 years.

148
00:09:12,420 --> 00:09:14,000
It's spread because it is food.

149
00:09:14,380 --> 00:09:16,020
It's a very durable food.

150
00:09:18,120 --> 00:09:22,020
An ounce of cheese contains seven times the protein of an ounce of milk

151
00:09:22,021 --> 00:09:23,820
and five times the calcium.

152
00:09:23,821 --> 00:09:28,820
cheese also delivers a concentrated wallop of fat calories.

153
00:09:28,821 --> 00:09:33,820
40 to 50% of its non-water weight is fat.

154
00:09:33,821 --> 00:09:39,820
Ancient cheeses were made predominantly from the milk of that hearty travel companion, the goat.

155
00:09:39,821 --> 00:09:46,820
They've been herded for over 7,000 years, and were one of the first animals to be domesticated.

156
00:09:46,821 --> 00:09:52,820
So I believe that probably means that goat cheese was some of the first cheese made in the world.

157
00:09:53,820 --> 00:09:57,760
In Homer's Odyssey, the founding epic of Western civilization,

158
00:09:58,640 --> 00:10:02,180
Odysseus' great foe, the Cyclops, is in fact a cheesemaker.

159
00:10:03,520 --> 00:10:06,440
He curdles his goat's and sheep's milk into curd,

160
00:10:07,260 --> 00:10:09,220
drains the cheeses in tightly woven baskets,

161
00:10:09,640 --> 00:10:11,060
and even cave-ages them.

162
00:10:13,600 --> 00:10:15,860
Handcrafted goat cheeses continue to thrive,

163
00:10:16,040 --> 00:10:18,460
not only in Greece and across the Mediterranean region,

164
00:10:19,000 --> 00:10:22,460
but also at the Redwood Hill Farm in Northern California.

165
00:10:22,461 --> 00:10:27,140
Today we're making feta in our traditional vat here.

166
00:10:27,141 --> 00:10:32,860
We have a thousand gallons of milk that we started with this morning.

167
00:10:32,861 --> 00:10:36,220
We've added the beneficial cultures.

168
00:10:36,221 --> 00:10:38,380
Then we added the rennet.

169
00:10:38,381 --> 00:10:43,180
And you can see now that it's not really milk any longer.

170
00:10:43,181 --> 00:10:48,900
It's a coagulated mass that's like a giant piece of tofu or jello.

171
00:10:48,901 --> 00:10:53,900
The cheese makers cut the curd into relatively large cubes

172
00:10:53,901 --> 00:10:57,900
and separate it from the whey.

173
00:10:57,901 --> 00:11:01,900
Right now we're putting the curd into the molds,

174
00:11:01,901 --> 00:11:05,900
and you can see we're packing it in so it will continue to knit

175
00:11:05,901 --> 00:11:08,900
together and become one piece of cheese.

176
00:11:08,901 --> 00:11:11,900
With feta, we don't add any pressure.

177
00:11:11,901 --> 00:11:14,900
It's just the pressure of the curds and the knitting together.

178
00:11:14,901 --> 00:11:17,900
They'll get turned at the end of today,

179
00:11:17,901 --> 00:11:20,900
and drain on the other side overnight,

180
00:11:20,901 --> 00:11:25,900
and then tomorrow morning they'll be put in a sea salt brine.

181
00:11:29,900 --> 00:11:33,900
Cheese's value was a lightweight, long-lasting survival food,

182
00:11:33,901 --> 00:11:36,900
increased as ancient Mediterranean cultures,

183
00:11:36,901 --> 00:11:39,900
particularly the Romans, perfected the art of making

184
00:11:39,901 --> 00:11:44,900
large wheels of salty, hard cheese that would last indefinitely

185
00:11:44,901 --> 00:11:46,900
and were easy to transport.

186
00:11:46,901 --> 00:11:49,400
There were a lot of Roman centurions and soldiers

187
00:11:49,401 --> 00:11:50,400
that had to be fed,

188
00:11:50,401 --> 00:11:52,900
and cheese was an integral part of their diet.

189
00:11:52,901 --> 00:11:54,700
So the Romans, wherever they went,

190
00:11:54,701 --> 00:11:56,900
they took herds of cows or goats or sheep

191
00:11:56,901 --> 00:11:59,900
or stole whatever ones were there when they got there,

192
00:11:59,901 --> 00:12:02,400
and they would milk them and make cheese.

193
00:12:02,401 --> 00:12:05,400
The most predominant Roman cheese has always been

194
00:12:05,401 --> 00:12:09,400
and is now a very hard and dry and oily sheep's cheese

195
00:12:09,401 --> 00:12:11,400
that we call Romano Pecorino,

196
00:12:11,401 --> 00:12:14,400
meaning a cheese made of sheep's milk from the Rome area.

197
00:12:16,900 --> 00:12:20,400
Other great Italian hard cheeses like Grana Padano

198
00:12:20,401 --> 00:12:24,560
and Parmigiano Reggiano have been made continuously

199
00:12:24,561 --> 00:12:28,900
for 1,000 years and probably longer.

200
00:12:28,901 --> 00:12:31,300
The Romans weren't the only ones to make giant wheels

201
00:12:31,301 --> 00:12:34,400
of firm, aged cheese.

202
00:12:34,401 --> 00:12:38,620
As early as the 11th century, high in the Swiss Alps,

203
00:12:38,621 --> 00:12:41,220
cowherds passed the summer in isolation,

204
00:12:41,221 --> 00:12:44,140
grazing their cattle on sweet mountain herbs

205
00:12:44,141 --> 00:12:48,380
and producing large blocks of hard cheeses, like Gruyere,

206
00:12:48,381 --> 00:12:52,500
which were essential to get through the long, harsh winter.

207
00:12:52,501 --> 00:12:54,980
The Swiss have produced Gruyere continuously

208
00:12:54,981 --> 00:12:58,600
for at least 1,000 years.

209
00:12:58,601 --> 00:13:02,540
Their greatest innovation, though, was full of holes.

210
00:13:02,541 --> 00:13:05,580
So why are there holes in Swiss cheese?

211
00:13:05,581 --> 00:13:08,220
And how did some hard-drinking medieval monks

212
00:13:08,221 --> 00:13:11,620
create some of the world's best and stinkiest cheeses?

213
00:13:14,140 --> 00:13:16,640
The Romans called their dry-pressed cheeses

214
00:13:16,740 --> 00:13:20,140
Cassius fermatus, meaning formed cheese.

215
00:13:20,240 --> 00:13:23,680
The name stuck, giving the Italians formaggio

216
00:13:23,780 --> 00:13:26,080
and the French formage.

217
00:13:26,180 --> 00:13:28,080
Cheese will return on Modern Marvels.

218
00:13:37,000 --> 00:13:39,700
Step inside a world-class cheese store,

219
00:13:39,800 --> 00:13:43,640
and you also enter a living, breathing, historical record.

220
00:13:43,641 --> 00:13:46,640
a pungent library of the world's great cheeses

221
00:13:46,641 --> 00:13:48,980
in all their glorious eccentricity.

222
00:13:50,380 --> 00:13:52,840
Whether it's in California's celebrity-friendly

223
00:13:52,841 --> 00:13:54,640
cheese store of Beverly Hills...

224
00:13:54,641 --> 00:13:55,760
We did it.

225
00:13:56,040 --> 00:13:56,980
It passed from Burgundy.

226
00:13:57,280 --> 00:13:59,000
...or the bustling Fairway Market

227
00:13:59,001 --> 00:14:01,420
3,000 miles away in New York City.

228
00:14:01,660 --> 00:14:03,840
I'm asking if this St. Andre is right.

229
00:14:04,560 --> 00:14:05,880
I don't put it out if it's not right.

230
00:14:06,020 --> 00:14:06,720
Yeah?

231
00:14:07,560 --> 00:14:10,800
Cheesemongers Steve Jenkins and Norbert Wobnig

232
00:14:10,801 --> 00:14:13,760
have been introducing Americans to Europe's cheese traditions

233
00:14:13,761 --> 00:14:15,100
for over 30 years.

234
00:14:15,940 --> 00:14:16,900
What else today?

235
00:14:17,700 --> 00:14:19,820
What are you doing with that boring piece of brie?

236
00:14:20,780 --> 00:14:23,780
You can almost grade any good cheese store

237
00:14:23,781 --> 00:14:26,420
by the smell when you walk in the door.

238
00:14:26,960 --> 00:14:31,000
If it doesn't have a really pungent odor,

239
00:14:31,460 --> 00:14:32,480
you're not in the right place.

240
00:14:33,400 --> 00:14:37,440
Cheese becomes stinky thanks to the work of bacteria and molds,

241
00:14:37,441 --> 00:14:41,020
which break down fats and proteins into volatile compounds

242
00:14:41,021 --> 00:14:42,760
that pack a powerful odor.

243
00:14:44,440 --> 00:14:47,820
The stinkiest cheeses were created by medieval Trappist

244
00:14:47,821 --> 00:14:49,100
and Benedictine monks,

245
00:14:49,360 --> 00:14:52,440
who brought great creativity to the final stage of cheese making,

246
00:14:53,220 --> 00:14:53,920
ripening.

247
00:14:54,860 --> 00:14:58,960
These are stinky cheeses because they're washed rind cheeses.

248
00:14:59,700 --> 00:15:04,300
These guys learned that if they would rub the surface of the cheese,

249
00:15:04,301 --> 00:15:06,080
and mostly these were cow's milk cheeses,

250
00:15:06,081 --> 00:15:13,400
via water or brine or wine or beer or brandy or grape juice.

251
00:15:13,700 --> 00:15:17,280
That would feed the natural bacteria that were on the outside of that cheese,

252
00:15:17,540 --> 00:15:20,480
naturally, from the air and from the milk inside.

253
00:15:21,180 --> 00:15:26,020
The monks acted as a bridge from the ancient world into the medieval world,

254
00:15:26,021 --> 00:15:30,540
and they kept alive this making of cheese, and they turned it really into an art.

255
00:15:30,541 --> 00:15:36,240
This is a traditional Trappist cheese made literally by the Trappist monks.

256
00:15:36,480 --> 00:15:37,620
It's called mon de carte.

257
00:15:38,140 --> 00:15:42,280
This is about as close to the original recipe as there is.

258
00:15:42,740 --> 00:15:52,120
And what the monks would probably eat with this are some dried meats, a lot of ale, and a couple of prayers.

259
00:15:52,121 --> 00:15:59,120
Trappist and other monastic chases,

260
00:15:59,121 --> 00:16:02,120
which date from as early as the 6th century,

261
00:16:02,121 --> 00:16:05,120
are the ultimate guys' food,

262
00:16:05,121 --> 00:16:09,120
a meal designed to go with really strong beer.

263
00:16:09,121 --> 00:16:11,120
A fresh cheese wouldn't have made it,

264
00:16:11,121 --> 00:16:13,120
wouldn't have been so good with those beers.

265
00:16:13,121 --> 00:16:15,120
A rock-hard, dry, chalky cheese

266
00:16:15,121 --> 00:16:17,120
wouldn't be as good with those beers.

267
00:16:17,121 --> 00:16:19,120
They came up with a recipe

268
00:16:19,121 --> 00:16:24,020
where cheese was stinky and gooshy and excruciatingly delicious

269
00:16:24,021 --> 00:16:27,040
in order for it to stand up to the black bread

270
00:16:27,041 --> 00:16:30,820
and the really strong beers and the other stuff that they ate

271
00:16:30,821 --> 00:16:32,440
that was so lusty in those days.

272
00:16:35,020 --> 00:16:37,100
Another legacy of the Trappist recipe

273
00:16:37,101 --> 00:16:40,120
is the famously odoriferous Limburger cheese.

274
00:16:42,880 --> 00:16:45,780
At the Chalet Cheese Co-op in Monroe, Wisconsin,

275
00:16:46,440 --> 00:16:48,080
master cheesemaker Myron Olson

276
00:16:48,081 --> 00:16:50,440
makes Limburger in the same handcrafted manner

277
00:16:50,441 --> 00:16:52,840
that the company has used for over a century.

278
00:16:55,260 --> 00:16:58,460
Once the curd is formed, it's hand-salted,

279
00:17:00,880 --> 00:17:02,820
then sent off to the ripening cellar,

280
00:17:03,160 --> 00:17:05,520
where it gets its trademark bacterial smear.

281
00:17:06,560 --> 00:17:09,060
What we're doing is putting a bacterial water

282
00:17:09,061 --> 00:17:11,100
onto the surface of the cheese.

283
00:17:11,880 --> 00:17:16,020
It's a mixture of water, salt, and a special bacterial linens,

284
00:17:16,021 --> 00:17:19,180
and then he's rolling them and smearing each side,

285
00:17:19,280 --> 00:17:20,300
each end of the cheese.

286
00:17:21,200 --> 00:17:23,020
The bacteria will start to grow,

287
00:17:23,420 --> 00:17:25,200
breaks down the protein of the cheese,

288
00:17:25,680 --> 00:17:30,220
causes it to go from a very acidy, crumbly type of cheese

289
00:17:30,221 --> 00:17:32,180
to where it becomes soft.

290
00:17:32,580 --> 00:17:34,520
It softens on the outside of the cheese

291
00:17:34,521 --> 00:17:35,700
and works its way in.

292
00:17:37,100 --> 00:17:39,360
Limburger is not necessarily the smelliest

293
00:17:39,361 --> 00:17:40,500
of washed rind cheeses.

294
00:17:41,180 --> 00:17:43,580
Perhaps it's the type of smell that people remember.

295
00:17:43,581 --> 00:17:51,420
Limburger's Brevibacterium linens is the same bacteria that's partially responsible for human body odor.

296
00:17:53,220 --> 00:18:00,020
Chalet cheese has been propagating this same bacterial culture on the pine boards of its curing cellars since the early 1900s.

297
00:18:01,680 --> 00:18:05,480
Today, Chalet cheese is the only company making Limburger in the United States.

298
00:18:06,400 --> 00:18:10,320
But in the early 20th century, Limburger was hugely popular.

299
00:18:10,920 --> 00:18:16,520
I've heard figures that at one point there was up to 10 million pounds of Limburger consumed in a year.

300
00:18:16,760 --> 00:18:21,013
We currently make 1 million pounds, and that's enough to keep everybody in the U.S.

301
00:18:21,014 --> 00:18:21,714
happy.

302
00:18:23,260 --> 00:18:27,000
If you think a bacterial smear is an unsavory way to ripen a cheese,

303
00:18:27,420 --> 00:18:29,700
you might want to stay away from mimolette,

304
00:18:30,040 --> 00:18:35,060
which has a rind that's seasoned by the dander and excrement of burrowing mites.

305
00:18:35,600 --> 00:18:36,780
This is mimolette.

306
00:18:37,340 --> 00:18:39,540
This was Charles de Gaulle's favorite cheese.

307
00:18:39,541 --> 00:18:45,280
and when we turn it over, they allow the mites to eat into the rind.

308
00:18:46,480 --> 00:18:52,380
Again, this is tradition and may in fact have some effect on the flavor.

309
00:18:53,500 --> 00:18:55,920
The cheese, oddly enough, is quite mild

310
00:18:55,921 --> 00:19:00,080
and is somewhere between a cheddar and an edam.

311
00:19:01,000 --> 00:19:05,260
A much more commonly used organism for ripening cheese is mold.

312
00:19:05,261 --> 00:19:09,200
The white rind and the great soft-ripened cheeses

313
00:19:09,201 --> 00:19:10,500
like brie and camembert?

314
00:19:11,240 --> 00:19:12,000
All mold.

315
00:19:12,700 --> 00:19:13,820
But go ahead and take a bite.

316
00:19:14,480 --> 00:19:16,620
The mold on cheese is cultivated carefully,

317
00:19:17,280 --> 00:19:19,220
nothing like the type growing in a musty basement.

318
00:19:20,100 --> 00:19:21,960
The French have been around a long time,

319
00:19:22,000 --> 00:19:23,880
and they've eaten a lot of mold in their day.

320
00:19:24,740 --> 00:19:26,580
In the same family as brie,

321
00:19:26,800 --> 00:19:30,600
these bloomy-rind goat cheeses known as crottin and camellia

322
00:19:30,601 --> 00:19:32,900
are maturing at the Redwood Hill Farm.

323
00:19:35,260 --> 00:19:41,760
Early in the process, cheese maker Jennifer Bice added a penicillium candida mold to the

324
00:19:41,761 --> 00:19:42,461
milk.

325
00:19:42,462 --> 00:19:49,500
During the ripening stage, that mold migrates to the surface of the cheese, since

326
00:19:49,501 --> 00:19:51,043
it needs air to live.

327
00:19:51,044 --> 00:19:55,820
The cheeses are turned so that gravity will evenly distribute the

328
00:19:55,821 --> 00:20:00,280
butterfat. This is the aging room where the camellia

329
00:20:00,281 --> 00:20:08,160
are aging, and you can see now that we have penicillin candida mold growth.

330
00:20:08,161 --> 00:20:15,440
The mold grows on the cheese, and then the mold actually composts the cheese from the

331
00:20:15,441 --> 00:20:22,160
outside in, and that's why in Camembert and Bree, we have it runny around the edge, and

332
00:20:22,161 --> 00:20:28,600
it works its way until the complete inner heart of the cheese is ripe and runny as well.

333
00:20:28,601 --> 00:20:36,320
The most famous moldy cheeses are the blue-veined varieties, like England's Stilton, Italy's

334
00:20:36,321 --> 00:20:43,700
Gorgonzola, and especially France's Roquefort, the legendary sheep's milk blue, aged in the

335
00:20:43,701 --> 00:20:47,360
Roquefort caves and spiked with mold taken from locally grown rye.

336
00:20:47,960 --> 00:20:52,580
Roquefort is a particular blue made in the Auvergne region of France.

337
00:20:52,581 --> 00:20:56,840
It's pure sheep's milk, and it has to be aged in the caves at Roquefort.

338
00:20:58,600 --> 00:21:01,800
At a certain point, the cheese is spiked.

339
00:21:02,520 --> 00:21:04,520
Spikes are pulled out, air gets in,

340
00:21:04,560 --> 00:21:05,920
and the blue begins to blossom.

341
00:21:06,760 --> 00:21:11,620
It's a particular blue mold called Penicillium roqueforti.

342
00:21:12,260 --> 00:21:13,600
Roquefort is totally unique.

343
00:21:14,000 --> 00:21:16,180
It tastes like no other blue cheese.

344
00:21:17,360 --> 00:21:19,220
Another unique cheese icon

345
00:21:19,221 --> 00:21:22,220
simply goes by the name Swiss in North America,

346
00:21:22,720 --> 00:21:24,860
but its Swiss creators call it something else.

347
00:21:26,040 --> 00:21:27,540
This is Emmentaler.

348
00:21:28,380 --> 00:21:31,200
Emmentaler is made in Switzerland, in the town of Emmental.

349
00:21:32,200 --> 00:21:36,800
The Swiss make one, and there's also one made on the other side of the border in France.

350
00:21:37,400 --> 00:21:40,080
But the real one is this one.

351
00:21:41,480 --> 00:21:45,040
It is about 200 pounds when it's made.

352
00:21:46,400 --> 00:21:47,740
It's killing you, isn't it?

353
00:21:48,580 --> 00:21:51,000
Okay, so where do the holes come from?

354
00:21:51,800 --> 00:21:55,320
What we do back when we were first making Swiss cheese,

355
00:21:55,321 --> 00:21:57,460
So we add a special bacteria to it.

356
00:21:57,660 --> 00:21:59,620
It's called a propionic bacteria.

357
00:22:00,640 --> 00:22:04,000
The propionic bacteria, which occur naturally in the milk,

358
00:22:04,460 --> 00:22:06,700
thrive in the unusually warm aging rooms.

359
00:22:07,360 --> 00:22:11,620
A warm room is held at between 70 and 80 degrees.

360
00:22:12,200 --> 00:22:15,980
And as it warms the cheese, this propionic bacteria will start to grow.

361
00:22:16,560 --> 00:22:17,960
And they'll form little colonies.

362
00:22:18,180 --> 00:22:20,680
These colonies will start producing carbon dioxide.

363
00:22:21,320 --> 00:22:25,220
And this carbon dioxide goes out to the cheese, starts pushing like a balloon.

364
00:22:25,320 --> 00:22:28,320
And the longer it's left in this warm condition

365
00:22:28,321 --> 00:22:31,320
and the more the colonies grow, the bigger the eyes get.

366
00:22:31,321 --> 00:22:34,320
This bacteria also imparts a flavor to the cheese

367
00:22:34,321 --> 00:22:37,320
that becomes part of what we consider a Swiss flavor.

368
00:22:37,321 --> 00:22:42,320
Flavor, after all, is what cheese is all about.

369
00:22:42,321 --> 00:22:44,320
And for millions of cheese lovers,

370
00:22:44,321 --> 00:22:47,320
it doesn't get any better than this.

371
00:22:51,320 --> 00:22:54,320
The penicillium molds found in brie or blue cheese

372
00:22:54,321 --> 00:22:58,220
or blue cheese, are related to the penicillin antibiotic,

373
00:22:58,221 --> 00:23:00,360
but not closely enough to ward off an infection

374
00:23:00,361 --> 00:23:02,320
in the cheese eater.

375
00:23:02,321 --> 00:23:04,320
Cheese will return on Modern Marvels.

376
00:23:15,280 --> 00:23:18,880
Welcome to the world of processed cheese.

377
00:23:18,881 --> 00:23:21,180
Processed cheese can be many things,

378
00:23:21,181 --> 00:23:25,940
from uniformly square slices to creamy sauces.

379
00:23:25,941 --> 00:23:29,120
But essentially, it's cheese that's been melted down,

380
00:23:29,121 --> 00:23:32,560
homogenized with the help of non-cheese ingredients,

381
00:23:32,561 --> 00:23:34,740
and reformed.

382
00:23:34,741 --> 00:23:37,960
The end product has longer shelf life than natural cheese,

383
00:23:37,961 --> 00:23:40,940
melts more evenly, and can be produced more cheaply.

384
00:23:43,700 --> 00:23:48,000
This process can be seen at Winona Foods in Green Bay, Wisconsin,

385
00:23:48,001 --> 00:23:51,120
which makes a number of processed cheese products.

386
00:23:51,121 --> 00:23:53,000
including a squeeze bottle cheese sauce

387
00:23:53,001 --> 00:23:57,260
and its painted cow brand of cheese in a can.

388
00:23:59,340 --> 00:24:01,600
Processed cheese begins with natural cheese,

389
00:24:01,980 --> 00:24:03,720
in most cases cheddar or Colby.

390
00:24:04,780 --> 00:24:08,640
Here at Winona, 40-pound blocks of white cheddar are shredded.

391
00:24:09,640 --> 00:24:12,100
The aim is to create as much surface area as possible

392
00:24:12,101 --> 00:24:15,320
for the chemical transformations that will take place in the blender.

393
00:24:17,080 --> 00:24:19,100
This blender is 6,000 pounds.

394
00:24:19,101 --> 00:24:24,660
Therefore, it will produce approximately 12,000 cans of finished product today.

395
00:24:25,260 --> 00:24:27,160
We add cheddar cheese to the blender.

396
00:24:27,740 --> 00:24:35,080
We also add emulsifiers, whey powder, fat, and water to blend into the slurry mix

397
00:24:35,081 --> 00:24:39,660
to make a homogeneous blend or processed cheese base.

398
00:24:41,000 --> 00:24:44,620
The key ingredients in any processed cheese are the emulsifiers,

399
00:24:44,621 --> 00:24:51,140
food additives that disperse fats and prevent their separation from proteins, even when melted.

400
00:24:52,020 --> 00:24:54,500
This will give processed cheese its smooth texture.

401
00:24:54,720 --> 00:24:59,440
What I'm holding here is emulsifying salt that's typically used in processed cheese manufacture.

402
00:24:59,820 --> 00:25:05,380
It can be trisodium phosphate, trisodium citrate, disodium phosphate, disodium citrate.

403
00:25:05,520 --> 00:25:10,500
It's added at the beginning of the process, and what that does is it breaks the fat globules down

404
00:25:10,501 --> 00:25:15,460
into smaller little fat globules that will end up being dispersed throughout the cheese matrix.

405
00:25:17,140 --> 00:25:20,720
The ingredients are simultaneously melted down and agitated.

406
00:25:21,180 --> 00:25:24,620
The other crucial steps in reconstituting the cheese matrix.

407
00:25:25,420 --> 00:25:30,360
And it makes almost a plastic homogeneous mass in the cooker,

408
00:25:30,540 --> 00:25:32,220
and it's very, very hot at that point.

409
00:25:34,460 --> 00:25:38,280
From now on, the processed cheese will remain smooth and homogenous.

410
00:25:38,281 --> 00:25:42,320
And whether it's a processed cheese spread or a slice,

411
00:25:42,820 --> 00:25:45,340
it will have supreme meltability.

412
00:25:47,920 --> 00:25:51,360
The first processed cheese was introduced in 1915

413
00:25:51,361 --> 00:25:55,020
by the man who would go on to impact the history of cheese

414
00:25:55,021 --> 00:25:59,700
more than any other individual, James L. Kraft.

415
00:26:00,680 --> 00:26:03,560
Kraft's cheese career began in Chicago in 1903

416
00:26:03,561 --> 00:26:07,440
when he invested $65 in a horse and cart.

417
00:26:08,280 --> 00:26:10,320
and began peddling cheese to local grocers.

418
00:26:11,560 --> 00:26:14,280
But Kraft wasn't satisfied with the available product.

419
00:26:14,840 --> 00:26:19,280
There was not a lot of consistency in terms of flavor

420
00:26:19,281 --> 00:26:22,700
and the quality of the cheese that was being produced at that time.

421
00:26:23,000 --> 00:26:26,760
And there was also a problem with the short shelf life of cheese.

422
00:26:28,240 --> 00:26:32,880
In 1911, Kraft began experimenting with a heat-sterilized cheese

423
00:26:32,881 --> 00:26:34,100
that could be sold in a can.

424
00:26:35,020 --> 00:26:37,840
But the heating caused the fats and proteins to separate.

425
00:26:38,280 --> 00:26:41,580
Eventually, Kraft discovered that constant stirring

426
00:26:41,581 --> 00:26:44,720
and the use of emulsifiers solved the problem.

427
00:26:46,580 --> 00:26:49,360
Kraft soon landed a contract to supply the U.S. Army

428
00:26:49,361 --> 00:26:52,600
with 6 million pounds of the non-perishable cheese

429
00:26:52,601 --> 00:26:54,020
during the First World War.

430
00:26:54,640 --> 00:26:56,740
And this was a significant contract

431
00:26:56,741 --> 00:26:59,200
that really helped J.L. Kraft develop the business.

432
00:27:00,380 --> 00:27:02,560
The business quickly became an empire.

433
00:27:03,620 --> 00:27:05,920
By 1923, J.L. Kraft was running

434
00:27:05,921 --> 00:27:07,940
the largest cheese company in the world.

435
00:27:08,280 --> 00:27:13,280
Then in 1928, Kraft introduced the soft, golden,

436
00:27:13,281 --> 00:27:17,280
processed cheese loaf with a mellifluous name, Velveeta.

437
00:27:17,281 --> 00:27:20,280
What was unique about Velveeta was the fact that this,

438
00:27:20,281 --> 00:27:23,280
the way byproduct was put back into the cheese

439
00:27:23,281 --> 00:27:25,280
while it was being made.

440
00:27:25,281 --> 00:27:28,280
And what the scientists found out was that it produced a cheese

441
00:27:28,281 --> 00:27:32,280
with an unusual creamy texture, and an additional benefit

442
00:27:32,281 --> 00:27:35,280
was the nutritional benefits that Velveeta offered.

443
00:27:35,281 --> 00:27:36,745
Velveeta offered.

444
00:27:36,746 --> 00:27:43,120
Incredibly, by 1930, 40% of all cheese eaten in America was made by Kraft.

445
00:27:44,580 --> 00:27:49,760
Kraft engineers also introduced all the major technologies used to make processed cheese,

446
00:27:50,720 --> 00:27:54,200
including the continuous screw-driven cookers still used today.

447
00:27:55,860 --> 00:28:01,720
As if that weren't enough, a Kraft scientist also revolutionized the surprisingly high-tech art

448
00:28:01,721 --> 00:28:03,320
of making cream cheese.

449
00:28:03,321 --> 00:28:06,320
In the 1940s, Oscar J. Link invented

450
00:28:06,321 --> 00:28:09,120
the centrifugal separation process for cream cheese,

451
00:28:09,121 --> 00:28:12,120
which allowed us to separate the whey from the cream cheese

452
00:28:12,121 --> 00:28:15,760
in 15 seconds versus the hours it had previously taken.

453
00:28:15,761 --> 00:28:18,280
He also invented a unique heating process,

454
00:28:18,281 --> 00:28:21,680
which allowed us to extend the shelf life of the product

455
00:28:21,681 --> 00:28:23,280
to 120 days.

456
00:28:23,281 --> 00:28:28,080
Cream cheese is a natural, not processed cheese.

457
00:28:28,081 --> 00:28:30,320
But making a perfectly creamy schmear

458
00:28:30,321 --> 00:28:35,320
is far from simple, and it's done without the use of cream.

459
00:28:35,321 --> 00:28:38,320
The key is using bacteria to carefully manipulate

460
00:28:38,321 --> 00:28:41,320
the electric charges present in the milk.

461
00:28:41,321 --> 00:28:44,320
Those charges normally in milk are all negative charges,

462
00:28:44,321 --> 00:28:47,320
are enough of them, are negative charges.

463
00:28:47,321 --> 00:28:50,320
And so like charges repel, they don't like to come close

464
00:28:50,321 --> 00:28:52,320
to each other, and they repel each other.

465
00:28:52,321 --> 00:28:55,320
That keeps them stable, and that's why milk stays on liquid

466
00:28:55,321 --> 00:29:00,320
and not a gel until the bacteria come along and produce some acid.

467
00:29:00,321 --> 00:29:05,320
that changes some of these charge groups on the protein to positive charges,

468
00:29:05,321 --> 00:29:08,480
and now they start to get attractive and start to stick together.

469
00:29:09,120 --> 00:29:14,280
When the charges are balanced, the mixture congeals into a perfectly creamy state.

470
00:29:14,820 --> 00:29:16,800
Not runny, not stiff.

471
00:29:18,180 --> 00:29:24,120
At that point, it's quickly heated, killing the bacteria and creating cream cheese.

472
00:29:24,121 --> 00:29:33,720
In 1940, Norman Craft, James' younger brother, designed the unique chill roll system to make

473
00:29:33,721 --> 00:29:37,980
the product he'd envisioned for years, pre-sliced cheese.

474
00:29:38,900 --> 00:29:46,920
The heated processed cheese would flow onto a large refrigerated cylinder, what was called

475
00:29:46,921 --> 00:29:51,700
a chill roll, and so it would create a sheet of processed cheese that was very quickly

476
00:29:51,701 --> 00:30:01,300
chilled and that sheet was then cut into ribbons and then the long ribbons were cut crosswise

477
00:30:01,301 --> 00:30:07,880
into sandwich shaped squares that were then stacked in stacks of eight slices usually

478
00:30:07,881 --> 00:30:13,480
and then wrapped and packaged in that way and it sounds simple but it was really quite

479
00:30:13,481 --> 00:30:14,889
innovative at the time.

480
00:30:14,890 --> 00:30:19,240
The design breakthrough is still the method for making processed cheese

481
00:30:19,241 --> 00:30:23,840
When the product was finally unveiled in 1950,

482
00:30:23,841 --> 00:30:27,480
it became a sensation and then a staple.

483
00:30:27,481 --> 00:30:30,460
Its familiar blend of cheddar with dairy and non-dairy

484
00:30:30,461 --> 00:30:34,080
ingredients even became known as American cheese.

485
00:30:37,600 --> 00:30:40,860
Today, individual cheese slices are still a hefty slice of

486
00:30:40,861 --> 00:30:44,200
Kraft's business. On an annual basis,

487
00:30:44,201 --> 00:30:49,240
Kraft produces about 7.2 billion American slices,

488
00:30:49,241 --> 00:30:56,240
And, you know, my rough calculations would have that stretching end-to-end stretch around the earth about 14 times.

489
00:30:56,241 --> 00:31:01,240
Kraft's Cheese Whiz for dozens of cheese dishes and snacks fast.

490
00:31:01,241 --> 00:31:06,240
It's a brand new idea in food, entirely different from anything you've ever had before.

491
00:31:06,241 --> 00:31:13,240
In 1952, Kraft introduced Cheese Whiz, a high-moisture processed cheese spread made with additional stabilizers.

492
00:31:13,241 --> 00:31:18,240
This tantalizing pasteurized processed cheese spread is grand in any sandwich.

493
00:31:18,241 --> 00:31:21,240
Cheese Whiz is primarily, here in the States,

494
00:31:21,241 --> 00:31:24,240
used as a topping cheese, as a sauce for vegetables,

495
00:31:24,241 --> 00:31:25,240
potatoes.

496
00:31:25,241 --> 00:31:27,240
Broccoli and potatoes are really the way that it's used.

497
00:31:31,240 --> 00:31:34,240
Over the years, processed cheese has taken some hits

498
00:31:34,241 --> 00:31:35,240
to its image.

499
00:31:35,241 --> 00:31:38,240
Critics argue that it's greatly limited the American palate,

500
00:31:38,241 --> 00:31:41,240
while putting the squeeze on natural cheesemakers.

501
00:31:41,241 --> 00:31:44,240
But it continues to thrive.

502
00:31:44,241 --> 00:31:47,240
It's even earned the admiration of at least one

503
00:31:47,241 --> 00:31:50,980
of at least one world-renowned cheese connoisseur.

504
00:31:51,040 --> 00:31:52,740
I mean, I grew up on Velveeta.

505
00:31:52,820 --> 00:31:54,080
I love Velveeta.

506
00:31:54,140 --> 00:31:56,420
I mean, a Dr. Pepper and a peanut butter sandwich

507
00:31:56,480 --> 00:31:59,520
and a big slab of Velveeta and some comic books,

508
00:31:59,580 --> 00:32:01,060
and I'm a happy boy.

509
00:32:04,160 --> 00:32:06,220
To countless millions of people,

510
00:32:06,300 --> 00:32:11,840
happiness is a hot pizza or a cool caprese salad.

511
00:32:11,900 --> 00:32:13,800
The secret to happiness?

512
00:32:13,860 --> 00:32:15,400
It's the cheese.

513
00:32:15,460 --> 00:32:17,240
Just ask her.

514
00:32:17,241 --> 00:32:28,760
In the early 20th century, America's traditional cheesemakers urged the federal government to label the newly modified cheese products as embalmed cheese.

515
00:32:29,440 --> 00:32:31,720
The government settled on processed cheese.

516
00:32:32,820 --> 00:32:35,160
Cheese will return on Modern Marvels.

517
00:32:39,720 --> 00:32:42,880
Quick, what's the most popular cheese in the United States?

518
00:32:43,760 --> 00:32:45,300
Cheddar? American?

519
00:32:46,000 --> 00:32:47,080
Guess again.

520
00:32:47,240 --> 00:32:50,680
That's right.

521
00:32:50,681 --> 00:32:55,840
Thanks almost entirely to our undying love of pizza, the once obscure Italian cheese

522
00:32:55,841 --> 00:33:01,700
known as mozzarella has stretched its way to the top.

523
00:33:01,701 --> 00:33:06,240
It's hardly surprising when you consider that Americans eat about 100 acres of pizza per

524
00:33:06,241 --> 00:33:07,240
day.

525
00:33:07,241 --> 00:33:13,000
Over a year, that's enough to cover every square inch of Manhattan with pizza.

526
00:33:13,001 --> 00:33:16,220
Twice.

527
00:33:16,221 --> 00:33:21,480
And so back at Wisconsin's Alto Dairy, they make pizzeria-grade cow's milk mozzarella

528
00:33:21,481 --> 00:33:23,680
around the clock.

529
00:33:23,681 --> 00:33:28,720
We make approximately 250,000 pounds of mozzarella per day.

530
00:33:28,721 --> 00:33:34,780
On an annual basis, that comes to 96 million pounds in a year's time.

531
00:33:34,781 --> 00:33:42,800
And to put it on pizza terms, we produce enough mozzarella per day to cover 500,000 pizzas.

532
00:33:42,801 --> 00:33:48,640
is known as a pasta filata, or stretched curd cheese.

533
00:33:48,740 --> 00:33:50,900
A machine called a cooker stretcher

534
00:33:51,000 --> 00:33:55,420
heats the curd to 136 degrees, melting it.

535
00:33:55,520 --> 00:33:58,040
Constant mechanical pulling turns the melted curd

536
00:33:58,140 --> 00:34:00,320
into a stretchy mass.

537
00:34:00,420 --> 00:34:02,820
When it leaves the mixer and cookers,

538
00:34:02,920 --> 00:34:07,420
it's got this massive stringy texture to it.

539
00:34:07,520 --> 00:34:11,500
And that's what people like to see.

540
00:34:11,501 --> 00:34:16,040
The hot curd is molded into blocks.

541
00:34:16,041 --> 00:34:19,140
To preserve mozzarella's soft, springy texture,

542
00:34:19,141 --> 00:34:22,580
the blocks are gently coaxed into a cool, salty canal.

543
00:34:25,440 --> 00:34:30,360
The four-hour brine bath haunts the cooking and fermentation.

544
00:34:30,361 --> 00:34:32,280
Once cooled, the blocks of mozzarella

545
00:34:32,281 --> 00:34:34,220
are packaged and aged for about a week.

546
00:34:37,200 --> 00:34:40,720
Much of the cheese then takes a run through the shredder

547
00:34:40,721 --> 00:34:43,220
and into a vacuum-sealed five-pound bag.

548
00:34:44,580 --> 00:34:48,920
The next stop is a 500-degree oven at your local pizzeria.

549
00:34:49,560 --> 00:34:52,900
To create a slightly browned, perfectly stretchy mozzarella,

550
00:34:53,940 --> 00:34:57,280
the cheesemakers not only have to melt and stretch the curd just so,

551
00:34:57,700 --> 00:34:59,980
they must also guide the starter bacteria

552
00:34:59,981 --> 00:35:03,080
into breaking down the tightly bound milk proteins,

553
00:35:03,580 --> 00:35:05,780
called caseins, as well as calcium.

554
00:35:05,781 --> 00:35:12,660
Because calcium is the glue that holds the casein structure together in cheese.

555
00:35:12,661 --> 00:35:17,120
If you don't remove some of that glue with acid during the make procedure, the fermentation,

556
00:35:17,121 --> 00:35:20,360
the proteins are held tightly and they can't stretch and flow.

557
00:35:20,361 --> 00:35:23,720
If you remove all of it, then it just falls apart.

558
00:35:23,721 --> 00:35:24,760
Here's how we would quantify it.

559
00:35:24,761 --> 00:35:26,640
We'd put a ruler by it like this.

560
00:35:26,641 --> 00:35:31,940
We'd grab some cheese and we'll see how high it goes up before the last strand breaks,

561
00:35:31,941 --> 00:35:34,460
which is right there, which was 13 inches.

562
00:35:34,461 --> 00:35:37,460
Try it one more time.

563
00:35:37,461 --> 00:35:39,460
Even a little more.

564
00:35:39,461 --> 00:35:40,460
16 inches.

565
00:35:40,461 --> 00:35:42,460
So we take a number of readings, average them,

566
00:35:42,461 --> 00:35:44,460
and that would give us our empirical reading

567
00:35:44,461 --> 00:35:46,460
for this particular pizza bake.

568
00:35:46,461 --> 00:35:49,460
This is pretty close to what many people would consider

569
00:35:49,461 --> 00:35:51,460
an almost perfect pizza.

570
00:35:51,461 --> 00:35:54,460
But the only way to really tell that

571
00:35:54,461 --> 00:35:56,460
is to get the human involved to taste it.

572
00:35:56,461 --> 00:35:58,460
So...

573
00:35:58,461 --> 00:36:04,420
It's good.

574
00:36:06,160 --> 00:36:07,740
Pizza's on, so dig in.

575
00:36:11,400 --> 00:36:13,740
Despite the runaway popularity of pizza,

576
00:36:14,280 --> 00:36:17,180
the essence of this remarkable cheese remains

577
00:36:17,181 --> 00:36:19,200
fresh mozzarella de buffalo.

578
00:36:20,560 --> 00:36:22,620
Say hello to the Asian water buffalo.

579
00:36:23,700 --> 00:36:26,080
This gentle giant, not the cow,

580
00:36:26,081 --> 00:36:29,120
is the original source of authentic fresh mozzarella.

581
00:36:33,540 --> 00:36:37,020
Ever since the Goths brought water buffalo to the Naples region

582
00:36:37,021 --> 00:36:38,380
in the early medieval era,

583
00:36:39,220 --> 00:36:41,780
Italian cheesemakers have been making mozzarella di bufala.

584
00:36:43,420 --> 00:36:47,920
We do not even know exactly when the tradition started,

585
00:36:48,380 --> 00:36:52,040
but we can say that at least 1,000 years ago,

586
00:36:52,480 --> 00:36:56,000
we have traces that water buffalo mozzarella existed

587
00:36:56,001 --> 00:36:58,500
already in the form that exists right now.

588
00:36:59,580 --> 00:37:03,840
Water buffalo milk is prized for its sweet, rich, and slightly gamey flavor.

589
00:37:05,000 --> 00:37:09,040
It's perfect for fresh mozzarella, which, unlike the pizza variety,

590
00:37:09,041 --> 00:37:14,480
is a non-aged, higher-moisture, higher-fat cheese meant to be enjoyed immediately.

591
00:37:15,840 --> 00:37:19,260
A small California cheese company named Bubalis Bubalis,

592
00:37:20,120 --> 00:37:22,200
after the water buffalo's scientific name,

593
00:37:22,660 --> 00:37:25,560
makes 600 pounds of fresh mozzarella every day.

594
00:37:26,000 --> 00:37:30,380
about what the alto dairy produces in four minutes.

595
00:37:35,380 --> 00:37:37,700
Once the curd enters the cooker's stretcher,

596
00:37:38,360 --> 00:37:42,860
the instincts and timing of the cheese maker are everything.

597
00:37:44,460 --> 00:37:48,020
He adds about 200 degree hot water to it

598
00:37:48,021 --> 00:37:52,120
so that the curd will again become soft and stretchable,

599
00:37:52,121 --> 00:37:54,180
and then he'll add salt to it.

600
00:37:54,181 --> 00:37:59,780
He will stretch it to the point where he feels that it is good to form it into individual balls.

601
00:38:02,600 --> 00:38:06,680
The automated arms of the cooker-stretcher are modeled on human hands,

602
00:38:07,240 --> 00:38:10,400
the traditional tools for stretching and tearing fresh mozzarella.

603
00:38:12,040 --> 00:38:16,760
And this is the precise moment when you cut the mozzarella.

604
00:38:16,761 --> 00:38:20,680
And this is why mozzarella is called mozzarella,

605
00:38:20,880 --> 00:38:24,400
because you mozzi, meaning you cut,

606
00:38:25,060 --> 00:38:28,640
because mozzarella means to cut in Italian.

607
00:38:29,040 --> 00:38:30,880
So that's the reason they call it like that.

608
00:38:32,480 --> 00:38:36,080
Both handmade and automated methods finish in the same way.

609
00:38:36,740 --> 00:38:38,220
They drop into ice water.

610
00:38:38,660 --> 00:38:41,600
The ice water, basically, by the shock of it,

611
00:38:41,601 --> 00:38:43,900
because the curd is pretty hot still,

612
00:38:44,000 --> 00:38:45,460
coming from the cheese mess.

613
00:38:46,760 --> 00:38:49,600
it will develop a skin on the outside,

614
00:38:49,601 --> 00:38:52,680
which is kind of typical for the mozzarella buffalo.

615
00:38:53,560 --> 00:38:56,040
Today we got a nice skin on the outside.

616
00:39:01,720 --> 00:39:05,280
Bubulus Bubulus also makes its own buffalo milk ricotta.

617
00:39:06,680 --> 00:39:08,520
Ricotta is unique among cheeses

618
00:39:08,521 --> 00:39:11,360
in that it's made entirely from the discarded whey,

619
00:39:11,361 --> 00:39:12,600
rather than the curds.

620
00:39:14,040 --> 00:39:15,360
This whey, of course,

621
00:39:15,361 --> 00:39:17,800
which is a leftover after we make the curd,

622
00:39:17,801 --> 00:39:22,580
is still contained a very large amount, of course, of protein.

623
00:39:22,581 --> 00:39:26,440
This way we then put into a separate tank, a ricotta tank,

624
00:39:26,441 --> 00:39:31,380
where we heat it up to 198 degrees.

625
00:39:31,381 --> 00:39:37,120
And at that point, we add some salt and some citric acid to it.

626
00:39:37,121 --> 00:39:39,960
The protein, which is in that way still,

627
00:39:39,961 --> 00:39:42,520
will coagulate one more time.

628
00:39:42,521 --> 00:39:47,940
So that coagulant is basically ricotta cheese.

629
00:39:47,941 --> 00:39:51,460
While ricotta has been made out of whey for centuries,

630
00:39:51,461 --> 00:39:55,300
the booming whey industry is just getting started

631
00:39:55,301 --> 00:39:59,200
as cheese forges ahead into a new health-conscious millennium.

632
00:40:02,580 --> 00:40:06,220
In 1970, Americans ate 1.2 pounds per capita

633
00:40:06,221 --> 00:40:07,940
of mozzarella cheese.

634
00:40:07,941 --> 00:40:12,460
By 2005, the amount had increased to 10.2 pounds.

635
00:40:13,360 --> 00:40:15,540
Cheese will return on Modern Marvels.

636
00:40:19,560 --> 00:40:23,280
For centuries, whey has been treated mostly as a waste product,

637
00:40:23,540 --> 00:40:25,420
once drained from the valuable cheese curd.

638
00:40:28,560 --> 00:40:32,460
Considering that 90% of the milk used in cheese making will end up as whey,

639
00:40:32,880 --> 00:40:34,080
that's a lot of waste.

640
00:40:34,980 --> 00:40:37,200
After all, it was good enough for a little Miss Muffet.

641
00:40:37,940 --> 00:40:43,980
Today that whey finds new life, thanks to operations like the one at the Alto Dairy,

642
00:40:44,420 --> 00:40:53,000
which uses a six-story high dryer to create whey powder at the rate of 250,000 pounds per day.

643
00:40:53,920 --> 00:40:57,240
This filter mat is one of the largest filter mat dryers in the world,

644
00:40:57,340 --> 00:41:01,920
and it will dry approximately 12,000 pounds of powder per hour.

645
00:41:01,921 --> 00:41:04,560
We're essentially spraying thick whey product that's

646
00:41:04,620 --> 00:41:07,760
approximately half water, half solids.

647
00:41:07,820 --> 00:41:09,860
We're spraying it in a stream of very hot air,

648
00:41:09,920 --> 00:41:11,860
approximately 400 degrees.

649
00:41:11,920 --> 00:41:14,340
And as that product is falling through the air,

650
00:41:14,400 --> 00:41:16,500
the moisture in it is evaporating off,

651
00:41:16,560 --> 00:41:19,700
and it is converting it into a powder.

652
00:41:19,780 --> 00:41:22,880
Whey powder consists of about 12% protein,

653
00:41:22,940 --> 00:41:25,540
the rest being mostly milk sugars and minerals,

654
00:41:25,620 --> 00:41:29,280
such as calcium and magnesium.

655
00:41:29,340 --> 00:41:31,920
This former waste stream will become

656
00:41:31,921 --> 00:41:37,740
blended into countless prepared foods, from bread to sausages. Among its many

657
00:41:37,741 --> 00:41:42,120
uses, whey can act as a sweetener or a thickening agent while providing

658
00:41:42,121 --> 00:41:47,580
essential protein and minerals. Whey proteins can be further concentrated

659
00:41:47,581 --> 00:41:54,040
into whey protein isolates, the ingredient of choice in fitness products.

660
00:41:54,041 --> 00:41:59,460
Whey isn't the only food getting a healthy makeover. Nutritional aspects of

661
00:41:59,461 --> 00:42:04,140
cheese itself are also expanding.

662
00:42:04,141 --> 00:42:07,600
There's no getting around the fact that cheese contains a lot of fat.

663
00:42:07,601 --> 00:42:12,960
That's just the nature of the beast.

664
00:42:12,961 --> 00:42:16,240
But scientists at the Wisconsin Center for Dairy Research

665
00:42:16,241 --> 00:42:19,740
are working with dairy farmers and cheesemakers to squeeze more nutrition

666
00:42:19,741 --> 00:42:21,060
from that fat

667
00:42:21,061 --> 00:42:24,700
by boosting cheese's levels of omega-3 fatty acids,

668
00:42:24,701 --> 00:42:26,960
the healthy fat.

669
00:42:26,961 --> 00:42:28,880
You can get it in two different methods.

670
00:42:28,881 --> 00:42:34,200
You can get it through a feeding regiment of feeding dairy cattle, a diet high in flaxseed,

671
00:42:34,201 --> 00:42:38,620
and what you get is an elevated level of ALA, omega-3 fatty acid.

672
00:42:39,160 --> 00:42:44,920
The other way to incorporate it is something seed-generated, either through fish or algae extracts.

673
00:42:46,300 --> 00:42:50,860
Scientists have even figured out how to isolate and remove any trace of a fishy smell.

674
00:42:53,100 --> 00:42:57,720
There's also increased interest in making cheese with beneficial probiotic cultures,

675
00:42:57,721 --> 00:42:59,480
like those found in yogurt.

676
00:43:00,120 --> 00:43:02,740
It's a big trend in Europe for a few years,

677
00:43:02,840 --> 00:43:03,920
but in the United States,

678
00:43:04,040 --> 00:43:06,220
it's just starting to take hold right now.

679
00:43:07,420 --> 00:43:09,560
Cheese, with its longer shelf life,

680
00:43:10,000 --> 00:43:11,840
is proving to be a good storage system

681
00:43:11,841 --> 00:43:13,200
for these healthy bacteria.

682
00:43:17,120 --> 00:43:19,420
Science and technology notwithstanding,

683
00:43:20,420 --> 00:43:22,540
cheese will always be shaped by the creativity

684
00:43:22,541 --> 00:43:24,300
of the individual cheese maker.

685
00:43:27,720 --> 00:43:31,020
Cheese will also continue to be shaped by the land

686
00:43:31,120 --> 00:43:34,520
and the animals from which it came.

687
00:43:34,620 --> 00:43:39,460
I find it amazing that the animal can take on all the bacteria

688
00:43:39,560 --> 00:43:41,460
in the air and all the yeast in the air

689
00:43:41,560 --> 00:43:44,940
and the esters from the wildflowers that it eats

690
00:43:45,040 --> 00:43:48,780
and the esters from the wild garlics and wild leeks

691
00:43:48,880 --> 00:43:51,580
translate it through the mammal lactation and into the milk

692
00:43:51,680 --> 00:43:53,320
and into the fat and into the cheese

693
00:43:53,420 --> 00:43:57,720
so that we can recognize them in terms of finished flavor.

694
00:43:57,721 --> 00:44:01,360
and what give cheese its complexity and its character.

695
00:44:01,361 --> 00:44:02,760
I think that's really interesting.

696
00:44:02,761 --> 00:44:04,060
It's magic.

697
00:44:04,061 --> 00:44:05,520
It's been around a long time.

698
00:44:05,521 --> 00:44:07,560
There's a reason for that.

699
00:44:07,561 --> 00:44:09,860
It's good food.

700
00:44:09,861 --> 00:44:14,940
God made man, man made cheese.

